From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 22:16:32 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ALSA: virtio: start a substream afresh after a system suspend

The ALSA core prepares a suspended substream again from the start of its
buffer: it resets hw_ptr and appl_ptr after ops->prepare(), and the driver
resets its indirect PCM state. Two pieces of state from before the suspend
survived.

The suspended branch of virtsnd_pcm_prepare() kept vss->hw_ptr, the
buffer position where the substream had stopped. The first pointer call
after the restart counted that position as played, so the core position
jumped ahead and the indirect state took the queued periods for that
much less than they were. The transfer then wrote into periods still
queued, which complete with more than one period's length: from then on
each completion moved the position by two or three periods. A playback
stream suspended one period into its buffer played at twice its rate
(7/3 and 3 times two and three periods in), the data written into the
queued periods never reached the device, and the device itself kept
completing one period of real time after another. The fast phase lasted
until two completions reached one pointer call, whose modulo-buffer
count then lost a buffer and left the stream with fewer periods queued.
Restart hw_ptr in both branches.

Only a substream the core was running is suspended through the trigger,
which stops it, and the synchronization that follows releases it on the
device. A substream that was prepared, paused or in an xrun is only
marked suspended in the core: its stream stays on the device, stopped or
prepared and with the periods already written to it, while the driver
neither marks it suspended nor forgets its messages. The suspend of the
virtio device that follows does not reset it, and the driver's freeze
does not run: virtio_uml only disables the rings, and the back-end then
resets a device that holds requests and keeps the streams of one that
does not. A paused stream met a reset device that refused the release of
a stream it did not know, at the next prepare and, for good, at the next
open; a stopped stream whose queue had drained met a device that kept it
and refused its prepare and its parameters.

Release the stream of such a substream on the device from a driver
suspend callback, while the control queue still works, and mark the
substream for reconfiguration. The device then holds no request when its
rings are disabled, whatever the back-end does with the device, and the
next prepare, or the next open, sets the parameters again.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 sound/virtio/virtio_card.c    | 23 +++++++++++++++
 sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.h     |  7 +++--
 sound/virtio/virtio_pcm_ops.c | 54 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 3 files changed, 81 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/sound/virtio/virtio_card.c b/sound/virtio/virtio_card.c
index a3e69a460b85faf09447b5343d9e9c137cadac21..56e3f49621ad1e9eacb84a2a41c6396db8096179 100644
--- a/sound/virtio/virtio_card.c
+++ b/sound/virtio/virtio_card.c
@@ -368,6 +368,28 @@ static void virtsnd_remove(struct virtio_device *vdev)
 	kfree(snd->event_msgs);
 }
 
+/**
+ * virtsnd_suspend() - Release the streams that a system suspend leaves on the
+ *                     device.
+ * @dev: Device of the VirtIO sound device.
+ *
+ * Context: Process context.
+ * Return: 0.
+ */
+static int virtsnd_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct virtio_device *vdev = dev_to_virtio(dev);
+	struct virtio_snd *snd = vdev->priv;
+	unsigned int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < snd->nsubstreams; ++i)
+		virtsnd_pcm_suspend(&snd->substreams[i]);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static DEFINE_SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS(virtsnd_pm_ops, virtsnd_suspend, NULL);
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM_SLEEP
 /**
  * virtsnd_freeze() - Suspend device.
@@ -431,6 +453,7 @@ static unsigned int features[] = {
 
 static struct virtio_driver virtsnd_driver = {
 	.driver.name = KBUILD_MODNAME,
+	.driver.pm = pm_sleep_ptr(&virtsnd_pm_ops),
 	.id_table = id_table,
 	.feature_table = features,
 	.feature_table_size = ARRAY_SIZE(features),
diff --git a/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.h b/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.h
index 0f3a433175e49a27e50ab8897329b765406a3958..fff17cfe7d169099a5049068a2ade322b6604842 100644
--- a/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.h
+++ b/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm.h
@@ -33,8 +33,9 @@ struct virtio_pcm_msg;
  * @xfer_xrun: Data underflow/overflow state (0 - no xrun, 1 - xrun).
  * @stopped: True if the substream is stopped and must be released on the device
  *           side.
- * @suspended: True if the substream is suspended and must be reconfigured on
- *             the device side at resume.
+ * @suspended: True if the substream was released on the device side by a
+ *             system suspend since it was last prepared, so that it must be
+ *             reconfigured on the device side at its next prepare.
  * @msgs: Allocated I/O messages.
  * @nmsgs: Number of allocated I/O messages.
  * @msg_last_enqueued: Index of the last I/O message added to the virtqueue.
@@ -126,4 +127,6 @@ int virtsnd_pcm_msg_send(struct virtio_pcm_substream *vss, unsigned long offset,
 
 unsigned int virtsnd_pcm_msg_pending_num(struct virtio_pcm_substream *vss);
 
+void virtsnd_pcm_suspend(struct virtio_pcm_substream *vss);
+
 #endif /* VIRTIO_SND_PCM_H */
diff --git a/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm_ops.c b/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm_ops.c
index 1091100a4c982b90e6a682e8a6e2548217b1012a..9f5646c752dbbdfb7fd7fb08c91be5bb9a79526b 100644
--- a/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm_ops.c
+++ b/sound/virtio/virtio_pcm_ops.c
@@ -282,7 +282,6 @@ static int virtsnd_pcm_prepare(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
 		}
 
 		vss->buffer_bytes = snd_pcm_lib_buffer_bytes(substream);
-		vss->hw_ptr = 0;
 	} else {
 		struct snd_pcm_runtime *runtime = substream->runtime;
 		unsigned int buffer_bytes = snd_pcm_lib_buffer_bytes(substream);
@@ -296,6 +295,14 @@ static int virtsnd_pcm_prepare(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
 			return rc;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The ALSA core restarts the substream at the beginning of the buffer
+	 * after a prepare, and so does the indirect PCM state below. The
+	 * position of the first I/O message must restart there too, also on
+	 * resume: the position the substream had when it was suspended points
+	 * into the middle of the restarted buffer.
+	 */
+	vss->hw_ptr = 0;
 	vss->xfer_xrun = false;
 	vss->suspended = false;
 	vss->msg_count = 0;
@@ -430,6 +437,51 @@ static int virtsnd_pcm_sync_stop(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/**
+ * virtsnd_pcm_suspend() - Release the stream of a substream on the device side
+ *                         before a system suspend.
+ * @vss: VirtIO PCM substream.
+ *
+ * The ALSA core stops a running substream through its trigger and then
+ * synchronizes it, which releases it on the device and marks it suspended. A
+ * substream that was prepared, paused or in an xrun is only marked suspended
+ * in the core: its stream stays on the device, stopped or prepared and with
+ * the periods already written to it, while the driver neither marks it
+ * suspended nor forgets those messages. The suspend of the virtio device
+ * that follows does not reset it: the back-end resets a device whose rings
+ * are disabled when it holds requests, and keeps its streams otherwise. The
+ * restarted substream then met either a device that did not know its stream
+ * and refused to release it, or a stopped stream that refused its parameters
+ * and its prepare, and could not be recovered, nor, for a paused one, opened
+ * again.
+ *
+ * Release the stream while the control queue still works. The device then
+ * holds no request when its rings are disabled, whatever the back-end does
+ * with the device, and the next prepare, or the next open, configures the
+ * stream again.
+ *
+ * Context: Process context. Takes and releases the VirtIO substream spinlock.
+ */
+void virtsnd_pcm_suspend(struct virtio_pcm_substream *vss)
+{
+	struct snd_pcm_runtime *runtime = vss->substream->runtime;
+
+	if (!runtime || runtime->state != SNDRV_PCM_STATE_SUSPENDED ||
+	    vss->suspended)
+		return;
+
+	vss->stopped = true;
+	if (virtsnd_pcm_sync_stop(vss->substream))
+		dev_warn(&vss->snd->vdev->dev,
+			 "SID %u: failed to release the stream for the suspend\n",
+			 vss->sid);
+
+	scoped_guard(spinlock_irqsave, &vss->lock)
+		vss->msg_count = 0;
+	vss->stopped = false;
+	vss->suspended = true;
+}
+
 /**
  * virtsnd_pcm_pb_pointer() - Get the current hardware position for the PCM
  *                         substream for playback.
